;;; A conclusion's variable that no premise binds stops the run at the rule's
;;; first firing — regression `assert-var-stops`
;;; (`corpus/regressions.toml`).
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; One fact `(p a)`, and a rule whose `:assert` writes `?y`, which its
;;; `:match` never binds: read literally, *if some p holds, every name is a
;;; q*.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; A conclusion's every variable is bound on every way its `:match` can hold,
;;; by a parameter, a premise or an `eq` / `neq` guard (Q-M1e2.37), and a rule
;;; that writes one nothing binds is refused at load, with a line naming the
;;; two explicit spellings of what it could mean: a premise such as
;;; `(is-a ?y T)`, every object of a type, or `(eq ?y ?y)`, every name of
;;; the program. Every command that loads the program exits 1 with that line
;;; (`ein test`: 2).
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answered ───────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; The program loaded, and root fired `r` on `(p a)`: every command that
;;; saturated root stopped there, exit 1 (`ein test`: 2, an `ERROR`), with the
;;; Python implementation's spelling, `unbound var ?y in :assert — bindings:
;;; {'x': 'a'}` (`ein saturate` quoted it as a `KeyError`), at `2eb483e0`,
;;; release and debug alike. A stop is no answer. The engine now refuses it
;;; at load, on every command.
;;;
;;;

(relation p Node :why "{?1} is a node")
(relation q Node :why "{?1} is a q")
(p a)
(rule r ()
  :match (p ?x)
  :assert (q ?y)
  :why "{?x} is a p")
